# Security Engineering Standards

Enforcement: **B** (prompt). Scanners/hooks are **C/D** when configured — LLM review does **not** replace them.

## Non-negotiables

- Never commit secrets, tokens, private keys, or production credentials.
- Never log passwords, session tokens, PANs, raw PII beyond policy, or auth headers.
- Validate and encode untrusted input at trust boundaries.
- Prefer deny-by-default authorization; check every sensitive operation.
- Least privilege for service accounts, DB roles, cloud IAM.
- No automatic production deploy or destructive data ops without human approval.

## OWASP-oriented checklist (adapt to stack)

| Risk | Engineering expectation |
|------|-------------------------|
| Injection | Parameterized queries / safe APIs; never string-build SQL/LDAP/OS commands from input |
| XSS | Context-aware encoding; avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` / raw HTML unless sanitized |
| CSRF | SameSite cookies + anti-CSRF for cookie-based session mutations |
| SSRF | Allowlist outbound destinations; block link-local/metadata IPs |
| Broken auth | Proven libraries; secure session/JWT handling; rotate/revoke |
| Access control | Object-level checks; no IDOR via sequential IDs without authz |
| Misconfig | Harden defaults; disable debug in prod; secure headers |
| Vulnerable deps | Run available scanners (OSV, npm audit, pip-audit, etc.) and record results |
| Integrity | Verify artifacts; pin versions intentionally |
| Logging/monitoring | Security-relevant events without sensitive payloads |

## Implementation rules

- Authn ≠ authz — authenticate identity, then authorize action on resource.
- Encrypt in transit (TLS); encrypt sensitive data at rest per policy.
- Secrets via vault/secret manager or env injected at runtime — never source control.
- Dependency changes: review changelogs for supply-chain risk.
- Security-sensitive diffs: Bhishma must evaluate; Swachalit adds regression tests where feasible.

## Evidence

When claiming “secure”:

1. List checks performed (manual + tools).
2. Record tool names, commands, exit codes.
3. Mark residual risk explicitly.
4. Never invent scan results.
